    {
      "type": "row", "title": "Incident interpretation & telemetry coverage", "id": 105,
      "gridPos": { "h": 1, "w": 24, "x": 0, "y": 69 }, "collapsed": false
    },
    {
      "type": "text", "title": "How to read a scaling incident", "id": 29,
      "description": "Operator guide for the authoritative gateway-owned scaling loop.",
      "datasource": { "type": "datasource", "uid": "grafana" },
      "gridPos": { "h": 10, "w": 12, "x": 0, "y": 70 },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "1. **Start with MIG target vs heartbeat-live.** The target is GCE provider intent. A temporary gap is convergence, not demand. Live above target commonly means deletion/TTL lag or a resumed standby.\n2. **Check weighted demand.** `Committed demand units` plus `Queued demand units` is what explains scale-out for large sandboxes; raw sandbox count does not.\n3. **Check readiness and release gates.** New capacity is intentionally unallocatable until golden-ready and release-compatible.\n4. **For scale-in, watch draining hosts.** Cordons should finish as removed or aborted. A persistent drain or any delete failure is actionable.\n5. **Correlate client impact.** Capacity rejections and worker create errors show whether convergence became an outage.\n\nNomad still schedules the worker system job, but **Nomad Autoscaler is retired**. The gateway alone writes the worker MIG size in both directions."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "text", "title": "Telemetry semantics and remaining gaps", "id": 30,
      "datasource": { "type": "datasource", "uid": "grafana" },
      "gridPos": { "h": 10, "w": 12, "x": 12, "y": 70 },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "**Authoritative at 10s:** provider MIG target, heartbeat-live workers, memory-weighted committed and queued demand, allocatable capacity, gateway scale-out/scale-in actions, drain state, create outcomes, and release-gate mismatches.\n\n**Federated from workers at 30s:** resource-pool and memory admission, create concurrency, lifecycle counters, golden readiness, boot phases, process uptime, scrape health, and release identity.\n\n**Not currently emitted:** create/restore request-duration histograms, host CPU/disk/network pressure, deploy annotations, or an explicit MIG-target poll failure counter. The MIG target series is intentionally absent until a provider poll succeeds; do not treat no-data as a zero target. Benchmark latency remains offline evidence in `docs/benchmarks.md`, not a live dashboard signal."
      }
    },

    { "type": "row", "title": "Public ingress", "gridPos": { "h": 1, "w": 24, "x": 0, "y": 80 }, "id": 106, "collapsed": false },

